        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3 text-gray-900">10. Dispute Resolution</h2>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 text-gray-900">10.1 Governing Law</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These Terms shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of Kansas, without regard to its conflict of law provisions, except that the Federal Arbitration Act, 9 U.S.C. §1 et seq., governs the interpretation and enforcement of §10.2 and §10.3. This choice of law does not deprive you of the protection of any law of your state of residence that cannot be waived by contract, as described in §10.5.</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 text-gray-900">10.2 Arbitration; Small-Claims Carve-Out</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>Please read this Section carefully. It affects how disputes between you and BreederHQ are resolved.</strong> In plain terms: most disputes between you and BreederHQ go to a neutral arbitrator instead of a judge or jury, and only on an individual basis. Either of us may use small claims court instead. Where the arbitration rules allow, a dispute is decided on written submissions without a hearing; any hearing is held by video or telephone, and an in-person hearing happens only if the arbitrator decides one is needed. If you are a Consumer, the arbitration follows the American Arbitration Association's consumer rules, any in-person hearing is held in the county where you live, and BreederHQ pays almost all of the arbitration fees. You can opt out of arbitration within 30 days, as explained in subsection (k). These terms also cover a dispute about something that happened before this version took effect for you, unless that dispute was already filed in court or arbitration by then, as explained in subsection (m). This Section does not cover disputes between you and another User, such as a dispute between a Buyer and a Provider about a sale.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(a) Agreement to arbitrate.</strong> Except as provided in subsections (b), (i), and (k) and in §10.3, any dispute, claim, or controversy between you and BreederHQ arising from or relating to these Terms, any other User Agreement, or the Services, whether in contract, tort, statute, or otherwise (a "Dispute"), will be resolved by binding arbitration on an individual basis. In arbitration there is no judge or jury, and court review of an arbitration award is limited, but an arbitrator can award on an individual basis the same damages and relief that a court could award on an individual claim.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(b) Small-claims carve-out.</strong> <strong>Notwithstanding the foregoing, either party may bring an individual claim in small claims court for any matter within that court's jurisdictional limits, in lieu of arbitration, for as long as the claim remains in that court and is pursued only on an individual basis.</strong> A small-claims action against you may be brought only in the county where you live. You may bring a small-claims action in the county where you live or in Kansas. Either party may elect small claims court under this subsection even after an arbitration has been filed, to the extent the applicable arbitration rules permit.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(c) Informal resolution first.</strong> Before starting an arbitration, the party with a Dispute must send the other a written notice describing the Dispute and the relief requested (a "Notice of Dispute"). You send a Notice of Dispute to legal@breederhq.com; BreederHQ sends one to the email address on your account. A Notice of Dispute from either party must identify the party sending it (for you, your name and the email address on your account) and describe the Dispute and the relief requested. It may be sent by the party itself or by the party's lawyer or other authorized representative; a notice sent by a representative must state that the representative is authorized to act for that party. For sixty (60) days after a Notice of Dispute is received, the parties will try in good faith to resolve the Dispute informally, and either may ask for a telephone or video conference. Any statute of limitations and any filing deadline is tolled while this informal process runs. If the Dispute is not resolved within those sixty (60) days, either party may start an arbitration.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(d) Administrator and rules.</strong> The arbitration will be administered by the American Arbitration Association ("AAA"). You are a "Consumer" for purposes of these Terms and the other User Agreements if you are an individual using the Services primarily for personal, family, or household purposes (for example, a Buyer buying a pet), and a Dispute between a Consumer and BreederHQ is a "Consumer Dispute." If you are a Consumer, or if the AAA determines that its Consumer Arbitration Rules apply to the Dispute, the AAA Consumer Arbitration Rules apply, and if this Section conflicts with those rules, those rules control. Otherwise, the AAA Commercial Arbitration Rules apply. The AAA's rules are available at adr.org. A single arbitrator decides each Dispute.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(e) Hearings; remote first.</strong> To the extent the applicable AAA rules allow, a Dispute will be decided on the parties' written submissions without a hearing. Any hearing will be held by video conference or telephone. An in-person hearing will be held only if the arbitrator decides that one is necessary. If the arbitrator orders an in-person hearing in a Consumer Dispute, it will be held in the county where you live, unless the parties agree to another place. If the arbitrator orders an in-person hearing in a Dispute that is not a Consumer Dispute, it will be held in Kansas, unless the parties agree to another place. Either party, including BreederHQ, may take part in any hearing, including an in-person hearing, by video conference or telephone. Where the AAA Consumer Arbitration Rules apply and give a party a hearing right this subsection does not, those rules control, as subsection (d) provides.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(f) Fees and costs.</strong> If you are a Consumer, your share of the AAA's filing, administration, and arbitrator fees will not exceed the amount the AAA Consumer Arbitration Rules assign to a consumer, and BreederHQ will pay all other AAA filing, administration, and arbitrator fees. If you show that paying even that share would be prohibitively expensive for you compared with bringing your claim in court, BreederHQ will pay it. For a Dispute that is not a Consumer Dispute, fees are allocated under the AAA Commercial Arbitration Rules, subject to any reallocation the arbitrator makes under those rules or applicable law. Each party bears its own attorneys' fees and costs unless the arbitrator awards them under applicable law, and any right to recover attorneys' fees or costs that you would have in court is preserved in arbitration. BreederHQ will not seek attorneys' fees or costs from a Consumer unless the arbitrator finds that the Consumer's claim or defense was frivolous or brought for an improper purpose.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(g) Arbitrator's authority; delegation.</strong> A court, not an arbitrator, decides (i) whether an agreement to arbitrate under this Section 10.2 was formed between you and BreederHQ or exists, including whether it has been superseded, rescinded, or displaced by another agreement, (ii) whether §10.3 is enforceable and how it applies, and (iii) whether subsection (j) is enforceable. Once a court has found, or the parties do not dispute, that such an agreement was formed and exists, the arbitrator has exclusive authority to decide any other question about the scope, interpretation, applicability, or enforceability of this Section 10.2, including any claim that all or part of it is void or voidable. The arbitrator may award declaratory or injunctive relief only in favor of the individual party seeking it and only to the extent necessary to provide relief on that party's individual claim. The arbitrator will issue a written decision stating the essential findings and conclusions on which the award is based. The award is final and binding, and judgment on it may be entered in any court of competent jurisdiction.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(h) Starting an arbitration.</strong> A party starts an arbitration by filing a demand with the AAA under its rules and sending a copy to the other party (for BreederHQ, to legal@breederhq.com), after completing the informal process in subsection (c).</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(i) If the AAA does not administer the Dispute.</strong> If the AAA declines or is unable to administer a Dispute, other than because of your failure to pay a fee you owe under subsection (f), the parties will try to agree on a substitute arbitration administrator within thirty (30) days. If they do not agree, the Dispute will be resolved in court under §10.4 instead of in arbitration. If you are a Consumer and BreederHQ does not pay, when due, a fee it owes under subsection (f), you may choose to resolve the Dispute in court under §10.4 instead of in arbitration.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(j) Mass filings.</strong> If twenty-five (25) or more demands for arbitration that raise substantially similar Disputes against BreederHQ, or by BreederHQ, are filed by or with the assistance of the same law firm, group of law firms, or coordinated organizations within any ninety (90) day period ("Mass Filing"), then: (i) the AAA will administer the demands in batches of up to one hundred (100), and, where the AAA's rules allow, each batch will be assigned to one arbitrator; (ii) each demand is still decided individually, on its own facts, and the claimant in each demand keeps every right this Section gives an individual claimant; (iii) any supplementary rules or fee schedules the AAA applies to multiple or mass case filings apply, and if this subsection conflicts with them, they control; and (iv) any statute of limitations and filing deadline for a demand in a later batch is tolled from the date its Notice of Dispute was sent until its batch is administered. If a court decides this subsection is unenforceable as to a Mass Filing, each demand in it proceeds individually in arbitration under the rest of this Section.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(k) Your right to opt out.</strong> You may opt out of this Section 10.2 by sending an email to legal@breederhq.com from the email address on your account within thirty (30) days after you first accept a version of these Terms that contains this subsection (k), stating your name and that you opt out of arbitration. Each opt-out applies only to the User who sends it and must be sent by that User or, for a User that is a business or other organization, by an individual authorized to act for it; an opt-out may not be sent on behalf of a group of Users. If you opt out, Disputes between you and BreederHQ will be resolved in court under §10.4, and §10.3 continues to apply to the extent applicable law permits. That includes a Dispute that subsection (m) brings under this Section, such as one about events before version 2.2 took effect for you: once you opt out, no agreement to arbitrate in an earlier version of these Terms applies to it. A Dispute that subsection (m) leaves under an earlier version, because it was already filed, is not affected by an opt-out. Opting out does not affect any other part of these Terms.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(l) Severability; survival.</strong> Except as provided in §10.3 and subsection (j), if any part of this Section 10.2 is found unenforceable, that part is severed and the rest of this Section continues to apply. This Section 10 survives the termination of your account and of these Terms.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(m) Disputes about earlier events.</strong> This Section 10.2, §10.3, and §10.4 apply to every Dispute that has not been filed in a court or with an arbitration administrator before version 2.2 of these Terms takes effect for you under §12, including a Dispute about events that happened, or claims that arose, before then. They replace, for those Disputes, the dispute-resolution terms of any earlier version of these Terms. A Dispute that was filed in a court or with an arbitration administrator before version 2.2 took effect for you continues under the terms that governed it when it was filed. Applying this Section to an earlier Dispute changes only how and where that Dispute is resolved; it does not change the rights and obligations that are in dispute, and it does not shorten any time limit for bringing a claim that had not run when version 2.2 took effect for you.</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 text-gray-900">10.3 Class Action Waiver; Preservation of Public Injunctive Relief</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">You agree to resolve disputes with BreederHQ on an individual basis and waive any right to participate in a class action lawsuit or class-wide arbitration. <strong>Notwithstanding the foregoing, this Section 10.3 does not waive (a) any right to seek public injunctive relief on behalf of the general public to the extent such waiver is unenforceable under applicable law (including without limitation California Civil Code §3513 and the rule in McGill v. Citibank, 393 P.3d 85 (Cal. 2017)), or (b) any statutory remedy that, under applicable law, cannot be waived as a condition of using a consumer service. If a court of competent jurisdiction determines that the waiver in this Section 10.3 is unenforceable as to any specific claim, that claim shall be severed and proceed in court, while all other claims shall remain subject to arbitration on an individual basis.</strong> A request for public injunctive relief that is not waived under this Section will be decided by a court under §10.4, not by an arbitrator, and the court proceeding on that request will be stayed until the arbitration of any individual claims between the same parties is complete.</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 text-gray-900">10.4 Venue</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For any Dispute not subject to arbitration, you agree to submit to the exclusive jurisdiction and venue of the state and federal courts located in Kansas, subject to the small-claims carve-out in §10.2(b), which keeps small-claims actions against you in the county where you live. If you are a Consumer and the law that applies to you requires that an action be brought, or that you be able to bring it, in the courts for the county where you live, then to the extent that law requires, you may bring the action in those courts and BreederHQ will bring any action against you only in those courts. In any court proceeding, BreederHQ may appear through counsel and may take part remotely wherever the court allows.</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 text-gray-900">10.5 State-Specific Provisions</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Where applicable law of your state of residence requires terms different from those above, the following state-specific provisions apply:</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(a) California Residents.</strong> The class-action waiver in §10.3 does not apply to claims for public injunctive relief brought on behalf of the general public under California law (including without limitation McGill v. Citibank, 393 P.3d 85 (Cal. 2017), and California Civil Code §3513). California Civil Code §1789.3 disclosures: BreederHQ LLC's contact for complaints is legal@breederhq.com. The Complaint Assistance Unit of the Division of Consumer Services of the California Department of Consumer Affairs may be contacted in writing at 1625 North Market Blvd., Suite N 112, Sacramento, CA 95834, or by telephone at (800) 952-5210.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(b) New Jersey Residents.</strong> If you live in New Jersey and are a Consumer (as defined in §10.2(d)), the following do not apply to you: in these Terms, the last item of §3.4 (Buyer Acknowledgment Regarding Displayed Credentials and Documents), §4.1 (No Liability for Transactions), §4.3(a) (Excluded damages), §4.3(b) (Aggregate cap), §4.4 (Release for Disputes Between Users), §5 (Indemnification), and §7B.4 (No Liability for Third-Party Platform Actions); in the Buyer Terms, the last sentence of §3.4 (Provider Insurance and Licensing), §10 (Indemnification), the first paragraph of §11 (Limitation of Liability) and, in §8.3 (Assumption of Risk), the statements that you assume risks and that BreederHQ has no liability; in the Marketplace Transaction Terms, the sentence in §4.5 (Animals and Meeting in Person) by which you assume risks, the statement in §7.3 (Fraud Reporting) that BreederHQ is not liable for losses resulting from fraudulent transactions, §7.5 (Release for Disputes Between Users), and the first paragraph and list of §11 (Limitation of Liability for Transactions); and in the Breeder/Seller Agreement and the Service Provider Agreement, §7 (Indemnification), §7A (Release for Disputes Between Users), and the "Animals and in-person contact" paragraph of §10. BreederHQ's liability to you, any obligation you have to indemnify BreederHQ, and whether you have released or assumed the risk of any claim, are instead determined under applicable New Jersey law.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(c) Massachusetts Residents.</strong> The class-action waiver does not apply to the extent that, under Massachusetts General Laws Chapter 93A or other applicable Massachusetts law, the right cannot be waived as a condition of using a consumer service.</p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>(d) Other States.</strong> Where this Agreement is inconsistent with mandatory consumer-protection law in your state, the mandatory law of your state controls, and the inconsistent provision of this Agreement is severed and shall not affect the enforceability of the remaining provisions.</p>
